  <revision rev="3" vrev="1">
    <srcmd5>5a9db5e11b3ec6072d9f804c734d0f06</srcmd5>
    <version>0.84.3</version>
    <time>1787656829</time>
    <user>anag_factory</user>
    <comment>Update to 0.84.3. This replaces sr#1373482, which I revoked: that request
carried the bump but not the packaging decision 0.84.3 forces.

0.84.3 adds dist/bundle, a 7.1 MB esbuild bundle of the same application, and
repoints package.json's &quot;bin&quot; and the &quot;./rpc-entry&quot; export at it (0.84.2's bin
was dist/cli.js and had no bundle at all). That matters here because the
bundle inlines its own copy of detectInstallMethod(), so
pi-disable-self-update.patch - which edits dist/config.js and exists to stop
`pi update` running `npm install -g` over the RPM-owned tree - does not reach
it. Left alone, the package would either ship 7.1 MB of dead unpatched
runtime while diverging from upstream's declared bin, or, if the symlink
followed bin, silently lose the self-update protection.

pi-use-modular-runtime.patch points both entry points back at the modular
tree (dist/cli.js, dist/rpc-entry.js), which upstream still builds and ships
- its own release note keeps the public library and legacy module paths on
the modular runtime - and %prep then deletes dist/bundle, which nothing
references any more. Patching the bundle instead was rejected deliberately:
its chunk filenames are content-hashed and change every release, so the patch
would need re-deriving on every bump.

Verified in the built RPM: no dist/bundle path is shipped, package.json
declares bin = dist/cli.js, /usr/bin/pi points at that same file,
dist/rpc-entry.js is present, and dist/config.js is the only copy of
detectInstallMethod() - patched to return &quot;unknown&quot;.

The changelog no longer credits the release with the bundled runtime's
startup improvement, which openSUSE users do not get.

Built green on openSUSE_Tumbleweed/aarch64: rpmlint 0 errors, 0 warnings,
0 badness; %check asserts the built CLI reports 0.84.3.</comment>
    <requestid>1373506</requestid>
